<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Justin I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 450–<a href="/years/527">527 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Justin I was a Byzantine emperor of the early sixth century, uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. According to the site, a major Aksumite naval expedition across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> was undertaken at his request. It defeated the Himyarite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhu-nuwas">Dhu Nuwas</a> and extended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>'s control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justin-i.jpg" alt="Justin I" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Justin_I_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Justin was born to a peasant family in the hamlet of Bederiana near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nis">Naissus</a> (modern Niš, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>), in the province of Dardania. He was of Thraco-Roman or Illyro-Roman descent and spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and only rudimentary Greek. According to legend he was a swineherd. As a young man he left Dardania with two companions to escape poverty and took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Illiterate on arrival, he joined the newly formed palace guard, the excubitors, campaigned against the Isaurians and the Sassanian Persians, and rose to be comes excubitorum, commander of the palace guard, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus">Anastasius I Dicorus</a>. He married Lupicina, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> described as a barbarian slave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Anastasius died childless on the night of 8-9 July 518. Amantius, the chamberlain, backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a> as successor and is said to have given Justin money to buy his support, but Justin used it for himself. He controlled a smaller but better group of soldiers and was proclaimed emperor in the Hippodrome. Amantius and Theocritus were executed nine days later. His wife took the name Euphemia, a sign of the couple's fervent Chalcedonian faith.</p><p>Justin was seen as an illiterate soldier with little knowledge of statecraft, and relied on advisers, above all his nephew Justinian, whom he adopted. Because Justin could not read or write, the palace made a wooden stencil spelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word LEGI, &quot;I have read&quot;. On 28 March 519 Patriarch John II accepted the formula of Pope Hormisdas, ending the Acacian schism between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Justin persecuted Miaphysites for his first three years, and in 523 issued a strict edict against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>.</p><p>He cultivated client states on the borders and avoided serious warfare until late in his reign. King Tzath of Lazica came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 521 or 522 to receive royal robes from him and to be baptised. In 526 an earthquake destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, with an estimated 250,000 deaths, and Justin sent money for relief and rebuilding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> wrote that Justin's mental faculties declined in old age. Justinian, consul in 521 and caesar in 525, took over more of policy, and Justin named him co-emperor and, on 1 April 527, his successor. Justin died on 1 August 527. His reign founded the Justinian dynasty, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> and three later emperors. The Cilician city of Caesarea was renamed Justinopolis in his honour in 525.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Justin I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 525 a major seaborne expedition, undertaken with the encouragement of the Byzantine Emperor <b>Justin I</b>, overthrew the Himyarite king Dhu Nuwas, who had persecuted the Christians of Najran, and installed a Christian client ruler in South Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The massacre provoked a response: encouraged by the Byzantine emperor <b>Justin I</b>, Kaleb led a large expedition across the Red Sea in 525, defeated the Himyarite army and killed Yusuf.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 520s King Gurgenes rose against Kavad I and appealed to the Byzantine Emperor <b>Justin I</b>, but when Persian armies invaded he fled with his family and nobles to Lazica, according to Procopius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The ageing <b>Justin I</b> raised Justinian to co-emperor of the Eastern Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bederiana. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=500" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Justin I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=500">What was here in 500 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
